        ":question/id": "c77e327a-089c-5238-8678-9dd2cc77832c",
        ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=30 x^{2} + 15$ at the point $(3,285).$\n\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=30 x^{2} + 15.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(30 x^{2} + 15\\right)=60 x\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(3,285):$\n\n$$\nm=f'(3)=180\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\nm'=-\\frac{1}{m}=- \\frac{1}{180}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-\\left(285\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{180}\\left(x-\\left(3\\right)\\right) \\\\ x+180\\cdot y&=51303.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
